INTRO = [
    "Euler's Gallery is a hall of physics exhibits in the Bullet Physics Playground. Its point is to be faithful to the physics: each exhibit should do what the real thing does, for the reason the real thing does it, and say how it knows. This report collects, exhibit by exhibit, how each one is simulated and how it was tested against theory, with the numbers.",
    "Every simulated number here comes from a run made for this report, on 15 October 2026, or from the exhibit's README, and the exhibit sections say which. Most theory values are worked out by the exhibits themselves from their own parameters; where a theory value, a fit or a comparison was worked out for this report instead (for example the Dzhanibekov flip period, the gyroscope's formula values, the fits to Galileo's ramp), the section says so. Each exhibit was run headless, from its own folder, with the start pause off (HOLD_S=0, which changes nothing in the physics; see section 3), and its console output was read against its README and its code.",
    "A first pass of this report found several exhibits with real problems (section 2.1 lists them) and many places where the documentation said something the code didn't do. Those were fixed the same day, and every exhibit that changed was run again: the exhibit sections describe the gallery as it now is, and section 2 lists what was found, what was done about it, and what is still open.",
]

FIXED_SIM = [
    ("Newton's cradle", "At 9600 physics steps a second the first strike lost a third of its energy. The cause: before each step, only contacts that would still be squeezed at the step's end if the balls moved freely were worked out, so a contact that was squeezed but already opening was skipped, and Bullet let the balls pass through, losing the stored compression. Now any contact squeezed now or touching within the step is worked out, and each ball is handed back on its hinge's circle, turned with its threads. The first strike is the same at every step rate from 1200 to 9600. The fast merging of the row (in about 15 s) was the same energy leak: now, with no damping the row never merges, and with the default damping it settles over minutes (its relative swing halves in about 80 s), as real cradles do (Hutzler et al.: 1-2 minutes)."),
    ("The chain fountain", "Links were woken and frozen once per drawn frame, so the physics depended on the display speed. All of that now runs per physics step, with a fixed 1/1800 s step; the output is identical at 0.25x, 0.5x and 1x. The 60° stall: links woken ahead of the pick-up had free ends and were dragged off, until the whole coil was loose in the jar and the hanging chain rested on the frozen links below; the last woken link is now held to the unmoved coil, and landed links stop colliding. It empties at every bend. A theory check against Biggins and Warner (2014) is now printed, and the README's height table is re-measured. A second pass found the coil laid with joints bent past their limits (304 of 1054 at 20°, plus upright links between layers), which Bullet snapped back when woken, kicking the chain: that, not the stiffness, was why the stiff chain's fountain was higher. The coil is now laid within the limits, the speed is the rate links land, and the pile's push can be measured directly (CF_PUSH=1). A third pass found why its fountain stood at half the measured height: the chain was laid as a coil. Poured into the jar as in the experiments, a ball chain of their size gives their fountain, in Bullet and in an independent bead-chain model; a live zone round the pick-up reproduces the fully simulated heap at 14 ms a frame at 1/8 speed, and the poured heap is now the exhibit (H switches to the old coil). Full account: exhibits/ChainFountain/FINDINGS.md."),
    ("The spinning eggs", "From 3° the left egg sat at the edge where friction holds its contact still, and the step's small errors decided when it broke free: 0.50 s at 4800 steps a second, 2.45 s at 19,200, never at 28,800 and 38,400. It now starts at 6°, twice that threshold, and rises at 0.30 s at every step rate; a new console check compares its rise with Moffatt and Shimomura's rate."),
    ("The spinning eggs (standing)", "Once standing, the left egg's tilt and fall-back depended on the step (tilt at 3 s 4.5°, 7.3°, 9.3° at 4800, 9600, 19,200 steps a second). The egg's own contact step imposed no-slip at the start-of-step contact point, though Bullet's update centres the velocities at mid-step; the gyroscopic term and the friction direction were first order too. The step is now second order and time-symmetric (no-slip half a step on, an implicit-midpoint gyroscopic term, Coulomb friction against the mean slip): 11.97-12.01° at 3 s and back on its side at 24.40 s at every rate, matching an exact rolling integration (reconstruction/rolling-check.py) to 0.04° and 0.01 s."),
    ("The solar system", "The yearly and monthly checks ran on the first frame after their date, and at high rates reached back to it inaccurately. The integration now stops at each check's exact date, runs the check, and goes on; and a second rate dependence in the moons' stepping was found and fixed. At 5, 60 and 100 days a second the check lines are now identical; at 100 days a second, Mimas on 2026-02-01 went from 4688 km to 41, Mercury in 2036 from 1220 km to 1. The README's samples are fresh."),
    ("Kapitza's pendulum", "At its old default of 4800 steps a second the rocking period was 1.7% short of converged, and that error happened to cancel the large-swing correction. The default is now 19,200 steps a second (converged to 0.1%), the console also compares with the period for the actual swing (1.133 s against 1.135 s), and a restart that misreported the size of the rocking (8.0° instead of 7.6°) is fixed."),
    ("The gömböcs and the Billes", "Their attendant's lift ran during the start pause, so with the pause on their output differed. The lift now waits for the pause to end, and the pause changes nothing in them either (checked with fixed seeds)."),
    ("The bicycle wheel", "A guard now checks every body's state each frame; if anything is not a number it prints the full state, the time and the move under way, and restarts the run. Pressing R mid-turn no longer prints a stray event."),
    ("The start pause (gyroscope, rattleback, tippe top)", "They timed their console lines by bpp's frame count, which includes the pause, so with the pause on their lines came at other moments (the physics was the same). They now count only the frames they run; all 21 exhibits pass the pause check. (Exhibits that start themselves again pause again each time, as the hall's README says, so their later lines come 0.5 s later per restart, unchanged.)"),
    ("The Brazil-nut effect (measurement)", "The floor's push was taken from the 10,000 kg box's change of velocity, which single-precision Bullet quantises to about 0.85 of the bed's weight per step; it is now the bed's own change of momentum, the big or coloured bead counted in the bed, and averaged over the shakes the text says (5-32; the old report covered 5-9.75). The 46-48° lift-off it reported was compared with the wrong theory: see section 2.3."),
    ("The Brazil-nut effect (rebuilt)", "An independent bead model (reconstruction/bead-bed.c, checked against a single bead's bounce, sliding on a slope and gentle shaking, and converged in step and stiffness) showed the Bullet version's pace was not settled (about 2x too fast at its default step) and its beads were not glass (friction 0.64 and 0.8, bounce 0.01). With real glass the rolls reverse and the big bead never rises in a shallow bed with smooth walls; a design study found a buildable one that works: a deeper bed, walls lined with glued glass beads, shaking at 5 Hz. Bullet can't run bouncy glass (its bounce boils a packed bed at any step), so the exhibit now runs the bead model itself through a new bpp hook (DemBed), which reproduces it bit for bit. Two boxes differ only in their walls: rough walls lift the big bead, smooth glass keeps it down. The old version's story is in exhibits/BrazilNut/reconstruction/FINDINGS.md."),
]
