METHODS = [
    ("How the exhibits are simulated",
     "Bullet (the Bullet Physics library, through bpp) carries the rigid bodies, joints and ordinary contacts. Where Bullet's contacts can't do the physics faithfully, the exhibit works it out itself and hands the result to Bullet each step: Newton's cradle integrates Hertz's law between the balls in quarter-microsecond sub-steps; the oloid and the spinning eggs work out their own contacts; the tippe top and the rattleback advance their spin by Euler's equations (RK4) with Bullet's gyroscopic term off; the gyroscope applies its own gyroscopic torque (its rotor is a number, a gyrostat, not a body spun at 100 turns a second). Some exhibits don't use Bullet's dynamics at all, and Bullet only draws them: Euler's disk integrates the exact rolling equations; the double pendulum, pendulums with a twist and the solar system integrate their exact equations of motion by Bulirsch-Stoer (Gragg's modified midpoint, extrapolated, tenth order), with RK4 for the shaken pendulums and Wisdom-Holman for the moons. The Brazil-nut effect's glass beads are simulated by a soft-sphere bead model in C++ (DemBed: Hertz-Mindlin contacts with Coulomb friction), not by Bullet, which can't handle bouncy glass in a pile. Each exhibit's section gives its integrator, its step and its parameters."),
    ("What 'tested against theory' means here",
     "Most exhibits print, as they run, the quantities theory predicts, next to what the simulation did: periods, rates, thresholds, conserved quantities, positions against an ephemeris. The theory values are worked out by the exhibit from its own parameters (so a change of slider changes both), or, for the solar system, taken from JPL. Some don't, or only partly: the rattleback's console is qualitative (its numbers are in a separate reference model), and the Brazil-nut effect is mostly qualitative; for these, and wherever else it helped, the comparison was made for this report and is marked so. Where possible a check is also run at a different step size, to see that the agreement isn't a numerical accident; the sections say where that was done and where it wasn't."),
    ("The hall and the exhibits on their own",
     "Each exhibit runs in its own physics world inside the hall, and the hall only runs the one you're at. Every exhibit's console output in the hall is compared, line for line, with its output when run on its own, and they are identical. This was checked for each exhibit as it was added, and again, for this report, for every exhibit whose console changed in the fixes (Newton's cradle, the chain fountain, the solar system, Kapitza's pendulum, the Brazil-nut effect, the bicycle wheel, and the exhibits whose text changed). The exceptions are the Gömböc and Bille drops, which are seeded from the clock and so differ from run to run anywhere."),
    ("The start pause",
     "Each exhibit stands still for half a second whenever it starts, so you see how it begins. During the pause bpp's own time step is zero and the exhibit's step is skipped, so the pause changes nothing in the physics: with the pause on, the output is the output without it, delayed, line for line. This was checked for all twenty exhibits that have it when it was added (at 2 s), and at 0.5 s for every exhibit changed in the fixes, including the gömböcs and the Billes with fixed seeds (their attendant's lift now waits for the pause to end). The runs in this report were made with it off."),
    ("Rendering and sound",
     "Bodies the physics moves are drawn where the physics has them. Some things are drawn without being simulated, and the sections say so: the chain fountain's heap away from the pick-up (fixed until links come within 4 cm of it) and its links once they land, the falling Slinky's pile once it has landed (laid to rest), the cradle's threads, the bicycle figure's arms, the cords of the swinging Atwood machines and springs (drawn as beads). The solar system's maps of each surface are drawn, not photographs (their markings placed where they are on the real body; the turning itself is the IAU's model, and is checked), and the gyroscope's tilt gauge turns twenty times as far as the wire does. Newton's cradle's clack is computed from the same Hertz strike, as the sound a jerked steel sphere sends into the air."),
]
